# Data licensing

The code in `src/`, `scripts/`, `js/`, and everywhere else outside `data/` is MIT —
see the root `LICENSE`. `data/` is not uniformly licensed the way code is: Belarusian
orthography data draws on several sources with different terms, tracked per file (and,
in two files, per row) by the `source`/`provenance` columns those files already carry.
This file is the index; several subdirectories carry their own fuller statement, which
this file summarises rather than replaces — where the two ever disagree, the
subdirectory's own README is the one to fix, and this file to update from it.

## 1. Wikidata-derived — CC0 1.0

Wikidata is public domain, https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/. No
attribution is legally required; each row carries one anyway, for traceability.

- `data/review/stem_candidates.tsv` — loan-stem candidates mined from paired Wikidata
  `be`/`be-tarask` item labels (`scripts/mine_wikidata_labels.py`). **Candidates only:**
  nothing here is applied by the converter until a human promotes a row into
  `data/lexicon/stems/`, at which point §4 below governs what it becomes.
- `data/names/w_names.tsv`, rows where `provenance` = `wikidata` (27 of the file's 32
  rows; the `evidence` column carries the Wikidata QID) — mined by
  `scripts/mine_w_names.py`.

## 2. be-tarask.wikipedia.org text, and text mined from it — CC BY-SA 4.0

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/. © the cited contributors; each row or
file names the article and revision id it came from, which is the attribution the
licence requires. A derivative built from this text — a Narkamaŭka rendering added
alongside a Taraškievica sentence, say — is distributed under the same licence, per
CC BY-SA's share-alike term.

- `data/eval/tarask/corpus.tsv`, `data/eval/tarask/gold_t2n.tsv` — see
  `data/eval/tarask/README.md`, the fuller statement this summarises.
- `data/corpora/parallel.tsv` — aligned be.wikipedia.org / be-tarask.wikipedia.org
  sentence pairs; see `data/corpora/parallel.README.md`. (The file's Narkamaŭka side is
  be.wikipedia.org text, also CC BY-SA 4.0 but from that wiki, not be-tarask — recorded
  here because the pairs are one artifact, not because that side is be-tarask-sourced.)
- `data/corpora/frequency_tarask.tsv` (be-tarask.wikipedia.org) and, for the same
  reason, `data/corpora/frequency_be.tsv` (be.wikipedia.org) — plain word-frequency
  counts, no running text.
- `data/names/w_names.tsv`, rows where `provenance` = `hand` (the remaining 5 rows) —
  read off attested sentences in `gold_t2n.tsv` above, so they carry that file's
  licence, not §1's.
- `data/eval/recall_misses.tsv`, `data/eval/roundtrip_failures.tsv` — generated
  reports (`pravapis eval --recall`, the round-trip property test), reporting on
  `data/corpora/parallel.tsv` and `data/eval/roundtrip_corpus.txt` respectively. Their
  `sentence` column quotes those corpora directly, so the quoted material carries
  whichever licence its source carries (CC BY-SA 4.0 here; MIT for
  `roundtrip_failures.tsv`, whose source is §4's `roundtrip_corpus.txt`) — the
  surrounding analysis columns (`cause`, `detail`, …) are the project's own, MIT.

### 2a. GrammarDB — also CC BY-SA 4.0, a different attribution

Not be-tarask text, but the same licence family, grouped here rather than invented as
a third top-level section. © Aleś Bułojčyk and Uładzimir Koščanka,
https://github.com/Belarus/GrammarDB.

- `data/stress/first_stressed.marisa`, `data/stress/proper_first_stressed.marisa` —
  see `data/stress/README.md`.
- `data/morphology/ment_lemmas.marisa` — see `data/morphology/README.md`.

## 3. Screened against be-tarask, but not derived from it

`data/review/stem_candidates.tsv`'s `verdict`, `evidence`, `blast_radius` and
`examples` columns report facts obtained by checking a Wikidata-derived candidate
against `data/corpora/frequency_tarask.tsv` (word *counts* — does the stem's converted
form or its original form appear, and how often — not running text) and against a
handful of illustrative sentences from `data/eval/tarask/corpus.tsv`. Reading a corpus
to test a hypothesis about it does not create a derivative work of that corpus: no
protected expression is copied into the row, only a fact about it. A candidate's
licence is therefore whatever §1 already made it (Wikidata, CC0) — screening it
against be-tarask does not pull it under §2.

The same reasoning covers `pravapis eval --recall` and `pravapis audit`, which read
`data/corpora/parallel.tsv` / `data/eval/tarask/corpus.tsv` to score the converter: a
score is a fact about the corpus, not a copy of it, and confers no licence obligation
on the code or on `data/lexicon/`. (`recall_misses.tsv` is the one report that *also*
quotes the corpus it scores — see the row for it under §2.)

## 4. Everything else — the project's own, MIT

Knowing that Збор 2005 §29 softens з/с before a soft consonant does not tell you which
words are loans, and citing a class rule by section number is not copying protected
expression from the codification. This is original classification and analysis work
by the project (Vladislav Hushcha), released under the same licence as the code — MIT,
per the root `LICENSE` — including:

- `data/lexicon/stems/stems.tsv` — **currently 100% `cited`/`reviewed` provenance**:
  every applied row cites a Збор 2005 section by hand, and none are `derived` today
  (see `pravapis.lexicon.stems.Provenance` — `derived` rows come from
  `scripts/mine_loan_stems.py`, GrammarDB paradigm mining, §2a's licence, not this
  one). The day a `derived` or Wikidata-promoted row is added, it is added *with* the
  licence its `source`/`provenance` implies, and this file's "When a stem's
  provenance changes" note below is what to update.
- `data/lexicon/{loanwords,exceptions,proper_nouns}.tsv`, `data/lexicon/case/*.tsv`
- `data/rules/*.yaml`, `data/translit/*.yaml`
- `data/morphology/function_words.tsv`
- `data/chars/*.tsv`
- `data/eval/gold.tsv` (Narkamaŭka side: Paznaj site content, author-owned;
  Taraškievica side: hand-written from it applying Збор 2005),
  `data/eval/roundtrip_corpus.txt` (also Paznaj content),
  `data/eval/roundtrip_regressions.tsv`, `data/eval/offset_cases.tsv`,
  `data/eval/unresolved_cases.tsv`, `data/eval/ambiguous.tsv`,
  `data/eval/negative.tsv`, `data/eval/known_fps.tsv`, `data/eval/baseline.json`
- `data/schemas/*.json`

## Not distributed, so not licensed by this file

`data/reference/` is git-ignored: copyrighted normative sources (the codification
texts themselves), kept locally for citation-checking, never redistributed — see
`data/reference/README.md`. `data/corpora/titles.checkpoint.jsonl` is a mining
checkpoint committed by accident (see `pravapis.conformance.EXEMPT_DATA`); nothing
reads it and it carries no claim either way.

## When a stem's provenance changes

`data/lexicon/stems/*.tsv`'s `source` and `provenance` columns are the record a
promotion has to update honestly: a `derived` row from `scripts/mine_loan_stems.py`
carries GrammarDB's CC BY-SA 4.0 (§2a), a row promoted from
`data/review/stem_candidates.tsv` carries Wikidata's CC0 (§1), and a `cited` row
citing Збор 2005 directly stays §4, MIT. This file is not regenerated automatically;
whoever promotes a candidate is responsible for updating this section — and the
promoted row's own `source` cell — to say which licence it brought with it.
